<?xpacket begin="ï»¿" id="W5M0MpCehiHzreSzNTczkc9d"?>
<x:xmpmeta xmlns:x="adobe:ns:meta/" x:xmptk="Adobe XMP Core 7.1-c000 79.cb7c5a1, 2022/04/14-05:22:35        ">
   <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#">
      <rdf:Description rdf:about="" xmlns:xmp="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:xmpMM="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/mm/" xmlns:pdf="http://ns.adobe.com/pdf/1.3/">
         <xmp:ModifyDate>2024-10-31T19:29:37+00:00</xmp:ModifyDate>
         <xmp:CreateDate>2022-10-10T12:06:49+05:30</xmp:CreateDate>
         <xmp:MetadataDate>2024-10-31T19:29:37+00:00</xmp:MetadataDate>
         <xmp:CreatorTool>Adobe Acrobat Pro DC (64-bit) 22.2.20212</xmp:CreatorTool>
         <dc:format>application/pdf</dc:format>
         <dc:description>
            <rdf:Alt>
               <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">DOI: https://doi.org/10.1093/heapol/czac058, Health Policy and PlanningAy, 00, 00, Published xx xxx xxx. Abstract: The unsustainable increases in healthcare expenses and waste have motivated the migration of reimbursement strategies from volume to value. Value-based healthcare requires detailed comprehension of cost information at the patient level. This study introduces a clinical risk- and outcome-adjusted cost estimate model for stroke care sustained on time-driven activity-based costing (TDABC). In a cohort and multicentre study, a TDABC tool was developed to evaluate the costs per stroke patient, allowing us to identify and describe differences in cost by clinical risk at hospital arrival, treatment strategies and modified Rankin Score (mRS) at discharge. The clinical risk was confirmed by multivariate analysis and considered patientsâ€™ National Institute for Health Stroke Scale and age. Descriptive cost analyses were conducted, followed by univariate and multivariate models to evaluate the risk levels, therapies and mRS stratification effect in costs. Then, the risk-adjusted cost estimate model for ischaemic stroke treatment was introduced. All the hospitals collected routine prospective data from consecutive patients admitted with ischaemic stroke diagnosis confirmed. A total of 822 patients were included. The median cost was I$2210 \absopenbrace {(}interquartile range: I$1163â€“4504). Fifty percent of the patients registered a favourable outcome mRS (0â€“2), costing less at all risk levels, while patients with the worst mRS (5â€“6) registered higher costs. Those undergoing mechanical thrombectomy had an incremental cost for all three risk levels, but this difference was lower for high-risk patients. Estimated costs were compared to observed costs per risk group, and there were no significant differences in most groups, validating the risk and outcome-adjusted cost estimate model. By introducing a risk-adjusted cost estimate model, this study elucidates how healthcare delivery systems can generate local cost information to support value-based reimbursement strategies employing the data collection instruments and analysis developed in this study.</rdf:li>
            </rdf:Alt>
         </dc:description>
         <dc:creator>
            <rdf:Seq>
               <rdf:li>Ana Paula Beck da Silva Etges</rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
         </dc:creator>
         <xmpMM:DocumentID>uuid:b9ea1aa0-661f-46cc-8bf0-a98fa7d44c87</xmpMM:DocumentID>
         <xmpMM:InstanceID>uuid:433b6dc2-4052-4655-b2a3-1476abadd187</xmpMM:InstanceID>
         <pdf:Producer>Adobe Acrobat Pro DC (64-bit) 22.2.20212; modified using iTextSharp 4.1.6 by 1T3XT</pdf:Producer>
      </rdf:Description>
   </rdf:RDF>
</x:xmpmeta><?xpacket end="w"?>
